-- title:
--   Fibrewise criterion: global sections of an invertible module are frames
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring, $X$ a scheme, $f : X \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ a morphism, and $M$ a module over $X$ which is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ such that the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit module (the structure sheaf) of $U$. Let $\sigma : \mathrm{Fin}\, n \to \Gamma(M, \top)$ be finitely many global sections of $M$. Two hypotheses are imposed for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every ring homomorphism $s_k : S \to k$, writing $X' = X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} S} \operatorname{Spec} k$ with projections $\mathrm{pr}_1 : X' \to X$, $\mathrm{pr}_2 : X' \to \operatorname{Spec} k$: first, for every point $x'$ of $X'$ there are a global section $t$ of $\mathrm{pr}_1^* M$ and an open $U' \ni x'$ with `IsFrameOn t U'`, i.e. for every open $W \le U'$ the map $g \mapsto g \cdot (t|_W)$ from $\Gamma(X', W)$ to $\Gamma(\mathrm{pr}_1^* M, W)$ is bijective; second, every global section $t$ of $\mathrm{pr}_1^* M$ is of the form $t = \sum_i c_i \cdot \mathrm{pr}_1^*\sigma_i$ for some $c : \mathrm{Fin}\, n \to k$, where $\mathrm{pr}_1^*\sigma_i$ denotes the image of $\sigma_i$ under the unit of the pullback–pushforward adjunction for $\mathrm{pr}_1$ and $k$ acts on sections of $\mathrm{pr}_1^* M$ over $\mathrm{pr}_1^{-1}(\top)$ through the ring map induced by $\mathrm{pr}_2$ and the inverse of the iso $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} k) \cong k$. The conclusion is that for every point $x$ of $X$ there exist an index $i$ and an open $U \ni x$ with `IsFrameOn (σ i) U`, that is, multiplication by $\sigma_i|_W$ is a bijection $\Gamma(X, W) \to \Gamma(M, W)$ for every open $W \le U$.
--
--   This is the fibrewise criterion for an invertible module to be generated by given global sections: if on every geometric fibre of $f$ the pulled-back sections span the global sections and the fibre is generated by global sections, then the $\sigma_i$ themselves trivialise $M$ locally on $X$. It is used in the construction of closed immersions defined by sections, in [`GoodReductionJacobian.AbelianSchemePropertyBundle.closedImmersionBySections_of_forall_geometricFibre_of_finite_of_forall_isPullback`](thm.html#GoodReductionJacobian.AbelianSchemePropertyBundle.closedImmersionBySections_of_forall_geometricFibre_of_finite_of_forall_isPullback).
